WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
Welcome back to GIS Resources. If you

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.760
are a surveyor or surveying student, you

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.240
already know that GNSS accuracy is not

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.080
controlled only by signal quality.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.200
One of the most important and often

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.280
misunderstood term factors is satellite

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.560
geometry, also known as dilution of

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.840
precision or DOP.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.200
In this video, we'll understand what DOP

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.600
really means from a surveying

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.720
perspective, how it is calculated

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.440
conceptually, and how each type of DOP

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.120
affects positional accuracy in the

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.440
field.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.240
What is DOP?

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.280
When a GNSS receiver calculates

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.560
position, it measures distances from

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.990
multiple satellites.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.320
But those distance measurements always

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.360
contain small errors caused by

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.240
atmospheric delay, clock bias, and

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:45.850
noise.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.960
Now, here is the key idea. DOP tells us

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.160
how satellite geometry amplifies those

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.440
measurement errors. A good satellite

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.280
geometry means small position error.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.440
A poor satellite geometry means large

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.840
position error.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.920
That means DOP is not an error source

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.160
itself.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.400
It is an error multiplier.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.120
Before we continue, if you haven't

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.400
watched our video GNSS signal

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.840
errors explained, clock, distance, and

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.000
noise,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.520
make sure you check that out after this

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.760
one.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.480
The link is in the I button and in the

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.960
description.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.840
Moving on, from a technical point of

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.560
view, GNSS positioning is based on

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.440
solving a system of equations using

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.640
satellite coordinates and

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.320
measured ranges.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.920
When these equations are solved using

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.800
least squares adjustment, we get a

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.920
covariance matrix. DOP values come

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.000
directly from this matrix.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.560
In simple terms, position error is

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.880
product of DOP and range measurement

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.240
error. So, even if your range error is

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.960
very small, a high DOP value will

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.840
increase the final positional error.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.800
This is exactly why surveyors always

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.720
monitor DOP before starting

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.400
observations.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.120
Let's start with GDOP, geometric

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.440
dilution of precision. GDOP represents

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.920
the combined effect of satellite

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.040
geometry on position accuracy and

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.320
receiver clock accuracy.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.320
Technically,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.600
GDOP includes the XYZ position errors

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.400
plus time error.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.000
So, it gives an overall quality

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.080
indicator of the satellite configuration

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.840
at a given time.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.120
A lower GDOP value means the satellites

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.800
are well distributed across the sky.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.800
Next is PDOP, position dilution of

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.920
precision. This is the most important

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.600
DOP value for surveyors. PDOP represents

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.960
the effect of satellite geometry on 3D

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.080
position accuracy, meaning XYZ and Z

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.520
coordinates.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.560
In practice, PDOP below three is very

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.371
good satellite geometry.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.400
PDOP between three and six is acceptable

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.080
for many surveys.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.720
PDOP above six is poor geometry and

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.800
reduced accuracy.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.600
When satellites are widely spaced in the

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.000
sky, PDOP becomes low.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.640
When satellites are clustered in one

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.880
region, PDOP increases significantly.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.760
This is why GNSS survey planning

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.520
software always shows PDOP values before

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.840
fieldwork. Now, let's separate position

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.000
accuracy into horizontal, HDOP,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.760
and vertical, VDOP, components.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.280
HDOP, horizontal dilution of precision,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.280
affects latitude

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.200
and longitude accuracy.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.760
This is especially important in

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.440
cadastral surveying, GIS mapping, and

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.440
topographic surveys. VDOP,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.600
vertical dilution of precision, affects

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.440
height accuracy.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.880
And as most of you already know from

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.160
field experience,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.600
vertical accuracy is usually weaker than

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.012
horizontal accuracy.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.320
Why?

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.160
Because satellites are located above the

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.560
observer, not below.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.000
This weakens the geometry in the

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.480
vertical direction and increases VDOP

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.400
values. That's why height

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.160
measurements often require longer

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.000
observation time or more precise

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.360
equipment.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.080
Next is TDOP, time dilution of

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.120
precision, which represents the effect

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.480
of satellite geometry on

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.840
receiver clock accuracy.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.840
GNSS positioning is completely

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.280
dependent on precise timing.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.160
Even a tiny clock error can create a

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.120
position error of several meters. TDOP

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.040
becomes important especially in high

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.000
precision positioning techniques like

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:22.720
RTK

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.520
and PPP, where time accuracy directly

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.960
affects positioning performance.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.640
So, to summarize,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.880
DOP does not create errors. It controls

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.360
how large those errors become.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.960
GDOP measures overall geometry. PDOP

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.280
controls 3D position accuracy. HDOP

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.120
affects horizontal accuracy. VDOP

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.400
affects vertical accuracy.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.520
And TDOP affects timing

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:50.880
accuracy.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.280
If this explanation helped you

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.960
understand satellite geometry more

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:54.753
clearly,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.600
like the video and share it with other

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.800
surveying students. See you in the next

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.320
video.
